REGENERATED WITH --instantiate=used --no_auto_instantiation
-----------------------------------------------------------
The committed C is now a SECOND translation, from the same commit and
the same cpfe recipe plus two flags.  What it fixes is the 556
template instantiations 6l reported undefined.

  WHY THEY WERE MISSING, from EDG's own source rather than from a
  guess: `DEFAULT_INSTANTIATION_MODE' resolves to `tim_none' here,
  because the build never defines INSTANTIATE_TEMPLATES_EVERYWHERE_USED
  -- and host_envir.h's own comment says the mode controls what is
  instantiated "without a specific request from the prelinker".
  DEFAULT_AUTOMATIC_INSTANTIATION_MODE is TRUE.  So cpfe was told to
  instantiate NOTHING and wait for an edg_prelink phase that this
  build never runs.  *That is a configuration assuming a two-phase
  build, not a limitation of the C back end* -- and it retires the
  recorded worry that `--one_instantiation_per_object' would be what
  introduced a prelink dependency: the default already had one.

  -tused emits every used template in every translation unit with
  EXTERNAL linkage, so it produces duplicates -- which is exactly what
  `pcc -C' now discards.  The two go together and neither is
  sufficient alone.  (-tlocal would give internal-linkage copies and
  need no -C, at a copy per unit; -C is needed either way for the
  non-template duplicates, which no instantiation mode touches.)

  MEASURED ON ONE FILE FIRST, because a full regeneration that changed
  nothing would have cost a round to discover.  src/interpret.c:

                          undefined templates   defined _Z
    committed recipe              39               226
    with the two flags             6               261

  and `edg::count_ones<unsigned int>' -- the exact symbol 6l named --
  goes from U to T.  All six survivors are DEFINED in other
  translation units (ordinary cross-TU references whose parameter
  types are templates), so the gap closes across the set.

  THEN THE WHOLE SET, the same sweep that reported 679:

                      before    after
    external            679       123
    _Z-prefixed         558         2
    template insts      556         0

  133 of 133 compile with gcc, 0 failures.  The two surviving _Z are
  the same non-template pair as before, a TLS init thunk and
  `operator new(size_t, align_val_t, nothrow_t const&)'.
  And `setjmp' rather than `_setjmp' now appears in the undefined set,
  so kencc_targ.h's TARG_SETJMP_FUNC line is confirmed and that gap
  closed with the same run.

  lib_src/ KEEPS ITS RECORDED RECIPE EXACTLY and is regenerated only
  so TARG_SETJMP_FUNC reaches eh_util.c and newnothrow.c.  It had no
  missing instantiations, `--building_runtime' is a different mode,
  and adding -tused there would be an unmeasured change to the
  runtime.  util/ gets the flags, being the same kind of program.

  util/cfe_daemon_client.c still does not translate and it is still
  the only one -- 133 produced, 1 failure, predicted by name before
  the run.

  AND THE FIRST ATTEMPT MEASURED NOTHING, for a reason worth keeping:
  the driver ran its workers under `xargs ... bash -c', and **bash
  cannot export an array**.  `export INC CPPDEF' was silently a no-op,
  every `--sys_include' vanished inside the subshell, and all 134
  files died at `bits/libc-header-start.h' -- glibc's headers, exit 4.
  The single-file test had passed minutes earlier in the shell that
  DEFINED the arrays.  *An instrument whose environment is not the
  build's environment is measuring a different program*, which is the
  include-path lesson arriving for a shell rather than a compiler.

THE LIBAP HALF OF THE LINK IS ANSWERED, AND IT IS TWO NAMES
-----------------------------------------------------------
Asked all at once rather than one per round, which is the rule bacon's
missing headers and bacon's missing link symbols both paid for: all
133 files compiled with `gcc -c' (133 of 133, 0 failures), then
`nm --defined-only -g' over every object subtracted from `nm -u' over
every object.

  symbols defined across the 133      22963
  undefined mentions                   9667
  TRULY EXTERNAL to the set             679
    of which `_Z'-prefixed              558   <- the link model, below
    of which plain C names              121

**Of the 121 plain names, libap already defines every one but two.**
Each was checked by opening the file, not by a grep hit -- and the
first sweep reported two dozen absent because its regex required a
character before the name, which a definition starting in column 0
does not have.  *That is the function-shaped grep of the `reject'
round in a new spelling, and it would have manufactured two dozen
findings.*  Present and verified: mmap/munmap (plan9/mman.c),
get/setrlimit, popen/pclose, sysconf, setlocale, posix_memalign,
mkstemp, pthread_key_create/setspecific, clock/ctime/difftime, isatty,
getcwd, isprint and the whole of stdio/string.

  _setjmp         lib_src/eh_util.c, lib_src/newnothrow.c
  __cxa_finalize  lib_src/static_init.c

`_setjmp' is a MISSED LINE IN kencc_targ.h rather than a libap gap:
EDG's `TARG_SETJMP_FUNC' defaults to "_setjmp" and win64 -- the same
configuration every other value here came from -- says "setjmp".  Set
now, so it is right at the next translation; the header records what
the miss costs and why adding `_setjmp' to a public APE header is the
wrong answer (lua/ldo.c already uses the name under LUA_USE_POSIX).

`__cxa_finalize' is NOT a libap question at all.  It is unconditional
in EDG's own `include_c++/cxxabi.h:184', nothing in lib_src defines
it, and `_Z12__call_dtorsv''s entire body is `__cxa_finalize(0)' --
upstream expects the platform's Itanium-ABI runtime to supply it.  On
a target that has none, the answer is one small APExp-owned file in
`sys/src/ape/lib/edg', where the C++ ABI belongs.  Not written: only
the link says whether cpfe references it.

Two more names are HOST ARTEFACTS and cannot appear from pcc:
`__stack_chk_fail' and `_GLOBAL_OFFSET_TABLE_'.  And the whole
libgcc soft-float family -- `__addtf3', `__multf3', `__eqhf2',
`__extendbfsf2', `__trunctfdf2' and twenty more -- comes from
`lib_src/c99_complex.c' ALONE, which is already predicted to fail on
`_Float16'/`__bf16'/`__float80'/`__float128'.  *The extended-float
types bring a soft-float library with them, and kencc has no libgcc*,
so that file is doubly out of reach and the count is not two findings.

Two confirmations fell out of the same table, and both say the staging
really used this tree: `_errnoloc' is undefined in six of src/'s
files and DEFINED in libap (`plan9/_envsetup.c:22'), which is APE's
`errno' macro from `<errno.h>' having been expanded; and `isNaN' --
Plan 9's own capitalisation, from `ap/math/nan.c' -- is referenced by
`src/float_pt.c'.  Neither name exists on glibc.

THE REMAINING LINK MODEL IS THE OPEN PROBLEM, AND IT IS TWO HALVES
------------------------------------------------------------------
**The 558 `_Z' names are NOT the duplicate problem seen from the other
side, and reading them that way was the round's one wrong sentence.**
They were demangled and counted rather than assumed:

  556 of the 558 are TEMPLATE FUNCTION INSTANTIATIONS
      edg::count_ones<unsigned int>, edg::index_to_str<...>,
      edg::pdqsort_impl::pdqsort_loop<...>, edg::is_named_decl<...>
  ZERO are vtables (_ZTV), typeinfo (_ZTI), typeinfo names (_ZTS),
      guard variables (_ZGV) or thunks
  the other 2 are a TLS init function and
      operator new(size_t, align_val_t, nothrow_t const&)

`_ZN3edg10count_onesIjEEyT_' appears in `src/interpret.c' as a call
and an `extern' declaration and **is defined in none of the 133
files**.  So cpfe emitted these instantiations NOWHERE: the output is
not self-contained for templates, *independently of
`--one_instantiation_per_object'*.  The paragraph below says OIPO
"makes the output DEPEND on a prelink step", which is true and
incomplete -- the DEFAULT output already does, for exactly the
entities OIPO was being considered for.  What the next translation
needs is an instantiation-mode flag (EDG's `-tused'/`--instantiate'
family) so the definitions are emitted; that will move work from this
half into the duplicate half below, where `ar' and `6l' semantics
decide rather than a missing symbol.

AND THE DUPLICATE HALF IS NOW MEASURED FROM OBJECTS RATHER THAN FROM
ANNOTATIONS, which is what makes the 3,094 below trustworthy:

  symbols DEFINED in more than one of the 133 objects   3124
  surplus definitions (sum of copies - 1)               6410
    _ZN... (inlines and emitted template members)       2616
    _ZZ... (function-local statics, incl. string lits)   499
    _ZTI/_ZTS (typeinfo and its name)                      4
    plain C names                                          5

3,124 against the 3,094 counted from COMDAT annotations is an
independent arrival at the same number by a different route, which is
better evidence than either alone.  `edg::skip_typerefs' is emitted
**41 times**, which is the shape: an inline reachable from a common
header.

  THE FIVE PLAIN NAMES ARE `main', `getopt', `optind', `optarg' and
  `opterr', and all five are confined to util/ -- `edg_decode',
  `edg_munch' and `edg_prelink' each bundle their own getopt, and the
  seven mains are src/cfe.c plus util's six programs, exactly as the
  file counts said.  **So nothing in src/ or lib_src/ is affected**,
  and cmd/edg compiles only src/.  But it is recorded because util/ is
  on the list: libap provides `getopt' and the three variables, so
  linking any of those three utilities against libap gives
  `redefinition: getopt' -- the f2c-supplies-its-own-malloc shape, and
  the reason an object on `_apemain''s path must define nothing a
  program might reasonably replace.

Removing __weak__ does not make the vague-linkage problem go away; it
stops hiding it.  Measured on two trivial translation units sharing
one header: with the kencc target they compile and then collide on 10
symbols -- B::f, three B::~B, typeinfo, typeinfo name, vtable, and
three Box<int> members.

Across src/ as shipped: 36,111 COMDAT annotations, 11,533 distinct
COMDAT symbols, and **3,094 of those are named in more than one of the
75 files**.  Those are what a kencc link has to resolve.  (An upper
bound on colliding definitions -- some annotations are declarations.)

`--one_instantiation_per_object` IS NOT USED HERE, deliberately.
Measured on the same two TUs, it removes the TEMPLATE instantiations
from the duplicate set (3 of the 10) and leaves the inline members,
vtable and typeinfo of an ordinary class (the other 7).  It does that
by NOT emitting them and listing them in a .ti file instead, for
`edg_prelink` to assign to exactly one object and schedule a separate
compile.  So OIPO is not a flag that makes the output self-contained:
it makes the output DEPEND on a prelink step.

  What that would mean for a Plan 9 mk build, stated and not built:
  mk would need a two-phase rule per package -- translate every .c,
  collect the .ti files, run edg_prelink over the whole set to decide
  which object owns each instantiation, then compile the extra
  generated files it names and only then link.  mk has no natural way
  to express "the set of objects is not known until after the first
  phase"; it would want a recursive mk or a generated mkfile
  fragment.  edg_prelink and edg_munch are both in util/ here, so the
  pieces exist.  NOT ATTEMPTED.

  The other half -- inline members, vtables, typeinfo of ordinary
  classes -- is NOT covered by OIPO at all.  Upstream's answer for a
  target without COMDAT is the Cfront-like ABI: host_envir.h:862's
  LINKER_CAN_DISCARD_DUPLICATE_DEFINITIONS, whose own comment is "a
  simplified interpretation of this flag is: do we have COMDAT
  sections?", forced TRUE by IA64_ABI.
  THAT WAS ATTEMPTED AND BACKED OUT, and where it stops is worth
  knowing: with IA64_ABI=0 plus the two settings its own #errors ask
  for (TIE_DEFAULT_GNU_ABI_VERSION_TO_GNU_VERSION,
  HANDLE_VIRTUAL_BASES_IN_COMPLETE_CTOR_DTORS), cpfe fails to COMPILE
  in src/target_map.h on three macros the Cfront ABI needs and the
  IA-64 ABI does not:
      TARG_ALIGNOF_PTR_TO_VIRTUAL_BASE_CLASS_linux_i686
      TARG_SIZEOF_PTR_TO_VIRTUAL_BASE_CLASS_linux_i686
      TARG_RUNTIME_ELEM_COUNT_INT_KIND_linux_i686
  This build is MULTI-TARGET -- the platform cmakedef declares
  TARGET_CONFIGURATION_1..7 and target.c instantiates the target map
  once per configuration -- and the generated cmake_defines.h carries
  the suffixed <MACRO>_<config> spellings only for macros reachable
  under IA64_ABI=1, which is what it was generated for.  Setting
  EDG_CPP_RT_LIBS="" does not help; that list is separate.
  Two of the three are mechanical (targ_def.h:2837,2842 derive them
  from TARG_SIZEOF_POINTER / TARG_ALIGNOF_POINTER) but the suffixed
  spellings must still be written out for all seven secondary targets,
  and TARG_RUNTIME_ELEM_COUNT_INT_KIND has no derived default at all.
  So the Cfront ABI is reachable by REGENERATING the macro
  configuration for it, not by setting switches -- and it is a large
  semantic change (mangling, ctor/dtor model, vtable layout) that
  wants its own round and its own validation.

APExp HEADER CHANGES THIS TRANSLATION DEPENDS ON
------------------------------------------------
At translation time the headers were STAGED (copied) and edited in the
staging copy only, and NOTHING IN APExp's HEADER TREE WAS MODIFIED.
Eight edits were needed, every one a case of an APE header not being
usable from C++.

**ALL EIGHT ARE FIXED IN THE TREE NOW, so a re-translation needs NONE
of them** -- stage the headers as they stand.  The list is kept
because it is the record of what a stricter compiler found, and
because each was fixed on its own evidence rather than to suit EDG.

The last four, each a parameter name or a keyword, so none of them
changes what a C caller sees:

  sys/include/ape/stdlib.h:47,49,87  `_Noreturn` (a C11 keyword that
      does not exist in C++) used unguarded
  sys/include/ape/stdlib.h:69,70     parameter named `template`
  sys/include/ape/unistd.h:186,192   parameter named `new`
  sys/include/ape/signal.h:161-164   `restrict` as a parameter name;
      two per prototype reads as a duplicate parameter name in C++

And the first four, fixed earlier in the same session for their own
reasons.  Verified against the tree rather than assumed:

  signal.h   `signal()` is prototyped now --
             `extern void (*signal(int, void (*)(int)))(int)` at :86
  signal.h   SIG_DFL/SIG_ERR/SIG_IGN are `(void (*)(int))` at :45-47;
             they had to move with the declaration, since callers
             compare the return value against them
  features.h `#define hidden` is GONE from the public header -- it had
             a measured victim in `sqlite3.h:10957`'s
             `unsigned char hidden[48]`
  bsd.h:53   `getopt(int, char * const [], const char *)`, POSIX's
             spelling, agreeing with `<getopt.h>` at last

*That is the translation's own staging list working as a to-do list
for the HEADER TREE*: it was written as eight things EDG could not
use, and every one turned out to be a defect worth fixing on its own
evidence, found by a compiler stricter than gcc.

The control is a before-and-after pair on IDENTICAL staging: a C++
translation unit including <stdlib.h>, <unistd.h> and <signal.h>
gives 12 errors before and 1 after.  The same unit as C is 0 errors,
which is the half that could have cost the tree.

THE SURVIVOR IS A NINTH ITEM THIS LIST NEVER NAMED: stddef.h:50
typedefs `wchar_t', which is a BUILT-IN TYPE in C++.  It is present
in both runs, so it is pre-existing rather than anything done here,
and it is recorded and not fixed.  cpfe does not trip on it --
`--no_standard_includes' plus EDG's own include_c99 is why -- so it
blocks nothing in this directory.

Two of the four could not be fixed the obvious way.  `#define
_Noreturn' or `#define restrict' under __cplusplus would put an
UNRESERVED name in a public APE header that external packages define
themselves: gnulib ships a whole `_Noreturn.h' and a dozen `config.h'
here define `restrict'.  That is exactly the collision that broke
flex over `reject' one round earlier, so the spellings used are all
in the reserved double-underscore space.  Three of the four need no
macro at all -- `restrict' becomes `__restrict', which kencc lexes to
the same LRESTRICT (cc/lex.c:1652,1678-1679), and the two parameters
are simply renamed.  `__ape_template' is this directory's own
convention arriving back in the tree: the generated C already carries
it.

One absolute include also had to be rewritten for a host compile:
amd64/include/ape/stddef_arch.h's `#include "/sys/include/ape/..."`.
Four others of the form `#include "/sys/include/..."` name native
Plan 9 headers and are unreachable from EDG, so they were left.

The four that remain are reported as findings in their own right and
are not fixed here; they are not EDG's problem either.


cpfe LINKED AND THEN CRASHED ON EVERY RUN, AND THE FAULTING ADDRESS
WAS `argc'
-------------------------------------------------------------------

`cpfe --help' answered

	cpfe 78213: suicide: sys: trap: fault read addr=0x2 pc=0x2

and `pc=0x2' is a JUMP to address 2, i.e. a transfer through a value
that is not code -- the same family as the recorded bash `pc=0x0'.

THE CAUSE IS A NAME COLLISION WITH THE LINKER'S ENTRY POINT.
`lib_src/main.c' defines `void _main(void)', EDG's static-init helper,
the one a target's own crt is expected to call.  `6l/obj.c:305' is
`INITENTRY = "_main"', and `sys/src/ape/lib/ap/arch/amd64/main9.s:3'
is `TEXT _main(SB), 1, $0'.  `6l' creates that name as an SXREF at
startup and satisfies it from the FIRST archive on the link line that
defines it -- and `cmd/edg/mkfile' names `libedg.a' ahead of libap.
So the program's entry became EDG's helper, which ran
`_Z12__call_ctorsv' (returning at once: `vars.c:49' has `__head' null
and `munch_ctors.c' defines `_ctors[1] = {0}', so the `_ctors' loop
terminates on its first test) and then executed a plain `RET'.

THE ARITHMETIC IS THE CONFIRMATION.  `main9.s' reads the kernel's
argument block at `0(SP)', so `0(SP)' at entry holds argc -- and that
is what the `RET' popped and jumped to.  `cpfe --help' is argc 2.
*A faulting pc equal to the argument count could not have arisen any
other way*, and it is falsifiable with no rebuild: `cpfe' alone must
have given `pc=0x1', `cpfe a b c' `pc=0x4'.

`-C' IS NOT IMPLICATED, which was worth checking rather than assuming
of the blunt flag standing next to it.  There was no duplicate for
`dupokall' to silence: `main9.$O' is pulled only to satisfy the entry
symbol, since its other global `_tos' is referenced from `profile.c'
alone -- so with `libedg.a' answering first that member never reached
the link.  First-archive-wins, and no linker has a diagnostic for it.

FIXED BY RENAMING RATHER THAN BY DROPPING THE OBJECT.  `lib/edg/mkfile'
compiles that one file with `-D_main=__edg_main'; nothing anywhere
references `_main', so removing the member from OFILES would have
linked equally well and would have thrown away the only entry point
into EDG's static-initialisation machinery.

THE STATIC-INIT GAP IS REAL AND IS RECORDED, NOT FIXED.  With `_main'
no longer the entry, nothing calls `_Z12__call_ctorsv' at all, so a
C++ program cpfe translates would not run its static constructors.
cpfe itself does not need it -- it runs, which is the measurement --
and cpfe does not emit a call to `_main' either: the name occurs in
the whole 133-file corpus exactly twice, both in `lib_src/main.c',
with no string literal of it in the emitter.  Upstream's convention is
a crt that calls it; this target's crt (`main9.s' -> `_callmain' ->
`_apemain' -> `main') does not.  The eventual answer is the APE
startup or the generated `main' calling `__edg_main'.

AND THE SWEEP IS WHAT SAYS `_main' IS ALONE -- one command against a
round of reading.  All 51 `lib_src' files through gcc, then
`nm --defined-only -g' over the objects, gives exactly FOUR external
definitions that are neither `_Z'-mangled nor `__'-prefixed:
`_ctors', `_dtors', `_main' and `_new_handler'.  The other three
collide with nothing in libap or kencc.  **`cmd/edg/mkfile' asserted
that every definition under `lib_src' is `_Z'-mangled or
`__'-prefixed, and that sentence is the one that would have found
this.**  It was a reading presented as a check, and an almost-true
claim is worse than none because it reads as having been verified.

AND THE FIRST BUILD OF THAT FIX DID NOT RUN: `=' IS AN rc SYNTAX
CHARACTER.  mk hands a recipe to `rc', where `=' is legal only as an
assignment at the start of a command or inside quotes, so a bare
`-D_main=__edg_main' is a parse error rather than an argument:

	/rc/lib/rcmain:24 *eval*:1: token '=': syntax error
	mk: pcc -c -C ...  : exit status=rc 79720: rc 79720: exit

The message names rcmain and a token and says nothing about this
mkfile.  Quoted.  Every other `-D' in the tree arrives through an
expanded `$CFLAGS', where mk substitutes after rc has parsed, so this
is the first LITERAL one here and had no working precedent in the
directory to copy from.

CONFIRMED: cpfe RUNS ON 9front.  After the rename it reaches its own
command-line parser and answers with its own diagnostics:

	$ cpfe --help
	Command-line error: invalid option: --help
	1 catastrophic error detected in this compilation.
	$ cpfe -V
	Command-line error: missing source file name

THAT IS THE ENTRY-POINT DIAGNOSIS CONFIRMED BEHAVIOURALLY, and both
messages are cpfe working rather than refusing.  `--help' and `-V' are
`eccp' DRIVER options; cpfe's own table (src/cmd_line.c) has no `help'
entry at all and spells the other one `--version'.  `-V' reaching
"missing source file name" means it was ACCEPTED and only the operand
was absent.

AND `--no_standard_includes' IS THE DRIVER'S TOO, which matters for
anyone writing the first command by hand: it is not in cpfe's option
table, because the driver implements it by simply not passing its
default --sys_include list.  cpfe has no standard include path of its
own -- it searches exactly what it is given -- so there is nothing to
suppress.

THE FIRST TRANSLATION ON 9front, as a command (from bash inside
apexp-sh; the `=' needs no quoting there, only in an mkfile recipe,
where rc parses it):

	cpfe --c++14 --no_exceptions --no_rtti \
	     --sys_include=<edg>/include_c++ \
	     --sys_include=<edg>/include_c99 \
	     --sys_include=/amd64/include/ape \
	     --sys_include=/sys/include/ape \
	     --gen_c_file_name=/tmp/t.c /tmp/t.cpp

Start with a toy that uses NO `new' and no static constructors: those
are the two places the runtime is reached, and the static-init gap
above is open.  `pcc -c /tmp/t.c' is the half that matters -- cpfe
emitting C says the front end runs, and pcc accepting it says the
target configuration in kencc_targ.h describes this machine.


THE C++ STANDARD LIBRARY: WHAT EDG GIVES AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
-------------------------------------------------------------

Asked as soon as cpfe ran, and worth settling before anyone starts: no,
EDG ships no standard library, and this directory contains none.

WHAT IS HERE, MEASURED.  `include_c++' holds ELEVEN headers and its own
README says exactly why: "only those headers that require specific
magic to work right with the EDG front end."  They are the
LANGUAGE-SUPPORT set -- <new>, <typeinfo>, <exception>, <stdexcept>,
<initializer_list>, <compare>, <coroutine>, <source_location>,
<stdfloat>, plus cxxabi.h and edg_internal_defines.  A grep across all
of them for `basic_string|vector|iostream|ostream' is EMPTY.
`include_c99' holds one file, `iso646.h'.

`lib_src' (51 files, 7191 lines) is the matching RUNTIME: the operator
new/delete families, vtables, RTTI, static initialisation,
pure-virtual, exception support.  **That is the libsupc++ / libc++abi
layer, not libstdc++.**  The distinction is the whole answer: EDG
supplies the compiler and the ABI runtime, and the standard library
proper is absent.

WHAT WORKS WITH NO LIBRARY AT ALL is more than it sounds, and the
evidence is this directory: classes, templates, inheritance, virtual
dispatch, operator overloading, RAII, new/delete, references,
namespaces, plus std::bad_alloc, type_info and initializer_list from
the headers above.  **That is the subset EDG ITSELF is written in**,
which is why the self-translation worked at all -- EDG has never used a
standard library, and `basic_hdrs.h' includes only EDG's own headers.

AND THE EXCEPTION MODEL IS THE FINDING THAT DECIDES THE REST.
EDG's EH here is **setjmp/longjmp with its own region bookkeeping** --
`__eh_curr_region' 138 occurrences, `an_eh_stack_entry' 99, `setjmp'
19, `longjmp' 2, and **ZERO references to `_Unwind_' anything** across
all 51 files.
  - GOOD: exceptions need no libgcc, no libunwind, no `.eh_frame' and
    no personality routine.  On a target that has none of those this is
    the only model that could work, and it is why EDG's C back end
    exists at all.
  - BAD FOR libc++: libc++'s EH integration assumes the ITANIUM ABI.
    So this is NOT the usual "drop libc++ onto a conforming ABI layer"
    arrangement that the libsupc++-shaped `lib_src' makes it look like
    -- the two EH halves genuinely disagree.  `-fno-exceptions' would
    sidestep it, at the price of a standard library that cannot throw.
*The layering LOOKS standard and the EH model is not, and only the
grep says so.*

NOT AN OBSTACLE, checked rather than assumed: the type-trait builtins a
modern STL needs are in cpfe's table -- `__is_trivially_copyable',
`__is_trivially_destructible', `__is_constructible', `__is_base_of',
`__underlying_type', `__is_aggregate', `__has_trivial_destructor' and
`__builtin_va_arg' all resolve.

THE CANDIDATES, AND THE AXIS THAT MATTERS IS NOT MAINTENANCE
------------------------------------------------------------
**APExp's stated goal is building existing UNIX software with minimal
modification.**  Against that goal the decisive property is the
NAMESPACE, not how alive the project is: software written against
`std::vector' and `std::string' does not compile against `etl::' or
`eastl::' however good those libraries are.  A renamed STL serves NEW
code written for it; only a `std::' implementation serves a PORT.
*That is a reasoning point and holds regardless of which project is
picked.*

  STLport      -- designed for weak compilers, EDG-based ones named
                  among them, which is the one axis where it is the
                  best fit here.  ABANDONED since 2008.
  ETL          -- actively maintained; EMBEDDED target, and its
                  containers are fixed-capacity (`etl::vector<T,N>'),
                  so it is a different API rather than a thin
                  substitute.  Own namespace.
  EASTL        -- more complete on containers and algorithms than ETL.
                  Own namespace; iostreams believed absent.
  uClibc++     -- the one candidate reported to use the `std::'
                  NAMESPACE and to carry iostreams, LGPL, aimed at
                  small systems and at compilers without full template
                  support -- which describes this target.  Open Hub
                  marks it INACTIVE at 0.2.5, and a 2009 Gentoo
                  developer post calls it broken and a source of
                  security problems.
  libstdc++    -- GCC-specific, built on GCC internals.  Worse than
                  any of the above here.

**PROVENANCE, because it decides how much weight each line carries.**
Everything above the candidate list is MEASURED in this tree by grep
and is reliable.  The candidate list is NOT: STLport's 2008 abandonment
and ETL's embedded fixed-capacity design are recalled and were not
re-verified; uClibc++'s status, licence and the 2009 criticism come
from one web search; and EASTL's namespace and missing iostreams were
NOT confirmed by search -- the result said "from general knowledge, not
from these results", so treat that line as unchecked.  *Anyone acting
on this list should re-check the project they choose rather than trust
this paragraph*, which is the same rule this tree applies to a stale
suite log.

THE HONEST ORDERING
-------------------
  1. NOTHING.  Treat APExp C++ as "C with classes and templates".
     Already reachable, needs no new code, and is strictly more than
     cfront ever delivered here.  Everything cpfe itself is written in
     works today.
  2. A SMALL STL in its own namespace (EASTL or ETL).  Buys containers
     and algorithms for NEW code; buys nothing for porting existing
     software, which is the project's stated purpose.
  3. A `std::'-namespace library (uClibc++, or STLport despite its
     age).  The only option that helps a PORT, and the one whose
     candidates are in the worst condition.
  4. libc++.  Large, C++20-era, wants <atomic>, threading and locale,
     and the EH model above is a real obstacle rather than a detail.

*Nothing here is started and none of it is a prerequisite for anything
already working.*  The next EDG step is still the one in the section
above: cpfe translating a toy and `pcc -c' accepting the C.


cpfe's TARGET IS NOT kencc's, AND kencc_targ.h NEVER REACHED target.c
----------------------------------------------------------------------

`cpfe --c++14 ... t.cpp' answers

	Internal error: check_target_config: must use SoftFloat library
	    for this configuration

and that is cpfe refusing its OWN configuration, not anything about the
input.  `src/target.c:10642' fires when `targ_ldbl_mant_dig == 64', an
80-bit x87 long double, which cpfe cannot constant-fold without a
SoftFloat library it was not built with.

cpfe IS MULTI-TARGET.  `target.c:6525' holds a table of EIGHT
configurations -- linux_x86_64, linux_i686, win64, win32,
linux_aarch64, linux_armv7, linux_riscv64, linux_riscv32 --
`fe_init.c:49180' defaults the index to 0, and `--target <name>'
(cmd_line.c) selects by name.  **Entry 0, labelled `linux_x86_64',
points at `set_legacy_target_config'** -- the configuration built from
the UNSUFFIXED TARG_* macros, which is exactly the set `kencc_targ.h'
overrides.

AND IT CARRIES THE HOST'S VALUES, measured from the generated C:

	set_legacy_target_config:  long 8  long double 16  mant_dig 64
	set_target_config_win64:   long 4  long double  8  mant_dig 53

So `kencc_targ.h' did not reach the translation of `target.c'.  **That
is a DIFFERENT macro environment from the one the NOTE above measured.**
The `__weak__' suppression (38,245 -> 0) is a property of the cpfe
BUILD -- how the host cpfe behaved; the TARG_* widths are read when
`target.c' is TRANSLATED, and only the second bakes into the committed
C.  The two were treated as one.
*Which mechanism is NOT settled*: the pre-include may have been absent
from the translation command, or present and then overridden by
`cmake_defines.h' included after it.  One look at the host build's
translation line separates them, and nobody should act on a guess here.

win64 IS kencc's INTEGER AND FLOAT MODEL EXACTLY, AND THE DIFF IS THE
WHOLE COST
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Both configurations are in the generated source, so their diff is a
COMPLETE list rather than a reading -- the `apeabi-probe' idiom again.
126 `targ_*' assignments each, **36 differ**, and they partition:

RIGHT for kencc, and legacy had them wrong:
  targ_sizeof_long 4, alignof_long 4          kencc long IS 32-bit
  targ_sizeof_long_double 8, ldbl_mant_dig 53,
    ldbl_max_exp 1024, ldbl_min_exp -1021     long double IS double
  targ_size_t_int_kind unsigned long long     APE stddef_arch.h
  targ_ssize_t_int_kind long long             APE stddef_arch.h
  targ_ptrdiff_t_int_kind long long           APE stddef_arch.h
  targ_setjmp_func "setjmp"                   <-- see below

**`targ_setjmp_func' is the corroboration.**  The NOTE above records
`_setjmp' as the one undefined symbol at the link and calls it "a
missed line in kencc_targ.h"; legacy says `_setjmp' and win64 says
`setjmp'.  *That the diff independently reproduces a bug found at the
LINK, from a completely different direction, is what says the diff is
measuring the right thing.*

WRONG for kencc -- the "windows baggage", and it is FOUR families and
one stray, not a vague risk:
  1. wchar_t / wint_t = unsigned short.  APE's `stddef.h:50' says
     unsigned INT.  Two bytes against four; definite mismatch, and it
     corrupts every L"..." literal and wchar_t layout.
  2. FIELD ALIGNMENT, five variables: double_field_alignment 4 (legacy
     8), long_field_alignment 4, long_double_field_alignment 4,
     dual_alignments_for_builtin_types 1, maximum_intrinsic_alignment
     8 (legacy 16).  **This is MSVC struct layout and it is the thing
     most likely to bite**, because kencc under `-J' aligns a double
     to 8.  Not yet measured; `structalign-test' is the instrument
     shape for it once anything compiles.
  3. BIT FIELDS: force_one_bit_bit_field_to_be_unsigned 1,
     zero_width_bit_field_affects_struct_alignment 1,
     zero_width_bit_field_alignment 4.
  4. POINTER-TO-MEMBER: sizeof_ptr_to_data_member 4 (legacy 8),
     alignof 4, delta_int_kind `short' (legacy `long').
  5. The one NON-`targ_' global the win64 config sets:
     `packing_applies_to_base_classes = 1'.

AND THE TWO MICROSOFT SWITCHES ARE OFF, checked because the name
invites the worry: `targ_microsoft_bit_field_allocation' and
`targ_microsoft_ptr_to_member_sizing' are both explicitly 0 in win64,
and the configuration sets NO mode flags at all -- 126 `targ_*'
assignments plus that one.  *`--microsoft' is a separate command-line
option and `--target win64' does not imply it.*

Watch `jmp_buf' too, recorded rather than resolved: win64 says 39
elements of `int', legacy 25 of `long', and APE's own is neither.
It matters here more than it would elsewhere because **EDG's exception
model is setjmp-based** (see the standard-library section above).

THE PREDEFINED MACRO FILE IS WHERE THE REST OF THE WINDOWS-NESS LIVES
---------------------------------------------------------------------
With `--target win64' the SoftFloat check passes and the next stop is

	catastrophic error: cannot open predefined macro file
	    "lib_win64/predefined_macros.txt"

`macro.c:19726''s `open_predefined_macro_file' builds
`edg_base_directory' + `auxiliary_info_dir_name' + the file name, so
the directory is per-target and we ship none of them.
**`--clear_flag=use_predefined_macro_file' is the answer, and it is not
a workaround**: that file is where a win64 build's `_WIN32' and
`_MSC_VER' would come from, so suppressing it is precisely "LLP64
without the Windows macros".  It is also the flag the NOTE's own
translation recipe already uses.

	cpfe --target win64 --clear_flag=use_predefined_macro_file \
	     --c++14 --no_exceptions --no_rtti \
	     --sys_include=<edg>/include_c++ \
	     --sys_include=<edg>/include_c99 \
	     --sys_include=/amd64/include/ape \
	     --sys_include=/sys/include/ape \
	     --gen_c_file_name=/tmp/t.c /tmp/t.cpp

THE REAL FIX IS THE LEGACY ENTRY, NOT win64.  `--target win64' is a
BRIDGE to a first translation with four named divergences; the correct
end state is index 0 carrying kencc's own values, which is what
`kencc_targ.h' was written for and demonstrably did not do.  That is a
re-translation with the pre-include actually in effect for `target.c',
and it would fix `wchar_t', the field alignments, pointer-to-member,
`jmp_buf' and `setjmp' in one go -- *every item on the list above is a
symptom of the same miss*.


AND THAT COMMAND FAULTS ON THE VM -- `fault read addr=0x68
pc=0x7e2d88' -- WHICH IS NOT A CONFIGURATION PROBLEM AT ALL
----------------------------------------------------------------

With the flag in place cpfe no longer refuses: it accepts the target,
accepts the option, and then dies inside real work.  *Which is itself
the first piece of information*: with `--target win64' alone the same
cpfe gave a clean `catastrophic error' about the macro file, so option
parsing and target selection are both past, and the fault is
downstream of them.

**THE HOST DOES NOT CRASH ON THE SAME COMMAND, which puts this on the
kencc/libap side rather than on EDG's.**  The host `cpfe' built from
the same commit with the same target configuration, given the same
flags and the same four `--sys_include' directories (APE's headers
staged), exits **0** and writes the expected C:

	/* Translated by the Edison Design Group C++/C front end */
	extern int __EDGCPFE__7_0;
	struct S; struct S { int x;};
	extern int _Z1fP1S(struct S *s); ...

So the two runs differ in the compiler that built cpfe and in nothing
else that has been identified.  *That is the same partition cfront's
crash had*, and there the answer was a layout rule.

THE STATIC-INIT GAP IS NOT THIS, AND IT IS NOW MEASURED RATHER THAN
ASSUMED.  It was the obvious suspect: `-D_main=__edg_main' means
nothing calls `_Z12__call_ctorsv', so a C++ global needing dynamic
initialisation would stay zero and be dereferenced -- exactly the
shape of a read at a small offset from null.  **One grep settles it:
the whole 133-file corpus contains exactly ONE static-init routine**,
`__sti___9_fe_init_c_2cbd8fba' in `src/fe_init.c:55031', and its whole
body assigns **0** to the sixteen members of
`edg::diagnostic_counters' -- values a BSS global already has.  So not
calling it costs cpfe nothing.  *The gap is real for programs cpfe
TRANSLATES and inert for cpfe itself*, and those are two different
claims; this one is now the measured half.

WHAT TO ASK NEXT, in order, because a `sys:' trap leaves the process
BROKEN and therefore readable:

  1. `ps | grep cpfe' -- it should still be there.
  2. `acid <pid>' then **`lstk()'**, not `stk()': this tree has twice
     had `stk()''s arguments name the wrong mechanism while the
     LOCALS named the right one.  Then `echo kill > /proc/<pid>/ctl',
     since a Broken process keeps its memory.
  3. `ls -l /tmp/t.c'.  If cpfe wrote any C before dying it reached
     the back end and the fault is in wrap-up; an absent or empty file
     puts it in the front end.
  4. The same command on an EMPTY `.cpp'.  Same `pc' means the input
     is irrelevant and the fault is in start-up; a different outcome
     means it is in translating this particular source.
